@implements IDisposable
@inject Announcer Announcer

@* The tile wall and the controls that page it.

   THE PAGER IS ABOVE THE GRID, and that ordering is the whole fix for a real problem rather than a layout
   preference. With 60 tiles on a page, a pager underneath means scrolling to the bottom, pressing Next, and
   then looking at the tail of the next page with the top of it a screenful above -- for every one of 25
   pages. Put the count and the pager first and the control the user just pressed is still in view, focus
   has not moved, and the new page's first tiles are immediately below it. The alternative was a
   programmatic scroll, which was rejected: it needs new interop and is one smooth-scroll option away from
   being motion the style contract cannot see. Reading order is h1, count, pager, grid.

   THE WHOLE HEAD IS GATED ON HAVING TITLES, count and pager together. Gating only the count left the
   pager asserting "Page 1 of 1" over a library with nothing in it, with no sentence anywhere to
   contradict it -- a page stating something false. Gating on PageCount > 1 instead would be worse: a
   filter that narrows the list to one page would then dissolve the pager while it holds focus, which is
   the case rule R9 exists for. The grid itself is gated with it, because an empty list of tiles is
   nothing a reader needs told about. Task 5 owns the "library is empty" sentence and Task 8 owns the
   "nothing matches these filters" one; neither of them is this component's to write. *@

@if (Items.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="grid-head">
        @* Plain text, and plainness is the point: this sentence is deliberately NOT a live region of any
           kind -- no polite or assertive attribute, no status, log or alert role. (Spelled in words
           because SourceContractTests scans this file for the literal markers and a comment quoting one
           fails exactly as the attribute would.) A page change is announced once, through the announcer's
           own hidden region; two regions reporting one change would speak it twice. *@
        <p class="count">@RangeSentence(CurrentPage, EnDash)</p>

        @* A second navigation landmark, which is allowed from this task onward: the shell's is
           nav[aria-label="Main"] and AppRoutingTests scopes its assertion to that one.
           No aria-label on either button. "Previous" and "Next" inside a landmark named Pagination are
           sufficient names in context, and adding a label would manufacture an SC 2.5.3 Label in Name
           subject for no user benefit. *@
        <nav class="pager" aria-label="Pagination">
            <button type="button" aria-disabled="@AtFirstPage" @onclick="() => Go(CurrentPage - 1)">Previous</button>
            <span>@PageLabelFor(CurrentPage)</span>
            <button type="button" aria-disabled="@AtLastPage" @onclick="() => Go(CurrentPage + 1)">Next</button>
        </nav>
    </div>

    @* role="list" is explicit and required. WebKit drops list semantics from a ul whose list-style is
       none, and display: grid on the container is the other known trigger -- this element has both. Task 2
       already made this call for the drawer's own ul. It is a document-structure role with no keyboard
       contract: all it does is give each tile a boundary and the wall a count, which is what stops a
       reader running 60 tiles together as one sentence. That is also why role="grid" and aria-rowcount
       are NOT here: those are composite-widget semantics and would promise arrow-key navigation that does
       not exist. If an li ever takes display: flex or display: contents, give it role="listitem". *@
    <ul class="grid" role="list">
        @* @key is on K and never on I. I is 0 for all 20 unmatched films, and a duplicate key within one
           collection fails the render. K's distinctness across the whole catalogue is pinned by
           CatalogueReconcilerTests.EveryKeyIsDistinctAfterTheRulesHaveRun,
           DuplicateKeyCollapseTests.EveryKeyIsDistinctAfterwards and EnrichRunCrossRowTests -- the last of
           which asserts it on the PUBLISHED json's k field, which is the artifact the browser actually
           reads. Hence no defensive de-duplication pass here. *@
        @foreach (var m in Items.Skip((CurrentPage - 1) * PageSize).Take(PageSize))
        {
            <li @key="m.K"><PosterTile Movie="m" /></li>
        }
    </ul>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The en dash in the VISIBLE range. The spoken range uses the word "to" instead, because how a screen
    /// reader pronounces a dash between two numbers is not something this page can decide.
    /// </summary>
    private const string EnDash = "–";

    /// <summary>The whole list this grid pages through. Task 8 replaces it with the filtered list.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required IReadOnlyList<MovieRecord> Items { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The page the parent wants shown, one-based. This component never writes to it: a component that
    /// mutates its own parameter fights the next render. Where the value is out of range the component
    /// renders the clamped page and raises <see cref="PageChanged"/> so the parent converges.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int Page { get; set; } = 1;

    /// <summary>
    /// How many tiles a page holds. Validated in <see cref="OnParametersSetAsync"/>, which throws below 1.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The brief said the arithmetic "cannot throw" and that is wrong twice over.
    /// <c>(Items.Count + PageSize - 1) / PageSize</c> divides by zero at <c>PageSize == 0</c>, because the
    /// <c>Math.Max</c> in <see cref="PageCount"/> is applied to the result and so cannot help. The worse
    /// case is NEGATIVE: at -1 with 1,456 titles, <see cref="PageCount"/> is 1, the first shown is 1 and the
    /// last shown is -1, so the head reads "Showing 1--1 of 1,456 titles." over a list holding no items at
    /// all. That is the same defect class as the empty-list "Page 1 of 1" -- a page stating something
    /// false -- reached by another route, and it is why this is a throw and not a clamp.
    /// <para>
    /// NOT <c>Math.Max(1, PageSize)</c>. That silently redefines the parameter: a caller asking for 0 would
    /// get a page of one, and a caller asking for -1 a page of one, when what they wrote cannot be honoured
    /// and the caller is the thing that needs fixing. At this component's scale it would also render all
    /// 1,456 tiles.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// A guard this component cannot make fail would be the unfalsifiable code rule T1 forbids, and an
    /// earlier pass left it out on those grounds. That was wrong: this is a public <c>[Parameter]</c>, so
    /// rendering the grid with <c>pageSize: 0</c> falsifies it in one line, which is what
    /// <c>PosterGridTests.APageSizeBelowOneIsRejectedRatherThanDividingOrLying</c> does.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public int PageSize { get; set; } = 60;

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised with the page the parent should adopt. The parent owns the page number, not this component,
    /// which is the seam that lets Task 8 lift it into the query string without touching this file.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<int> PageChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The out-of-range <see cref="Page"/> whose correction has already been raised, so the raise happens
    /// at most once per distinct bad value. Raising an <see cref="EventCallback"/> from a parameter hook
    /// loops for ever against a parent that recomputes <c>Page</c> rather than adopting what it is given,
    /// which Task 8's filter state plausibly will.
    /// </summary>
    private int? correctedFrom;

    /// <summary>
    /// Set by <see cref="Dispose"/>, read by <see cref="Go"/>. A parent may unmount this grid from inside
    /// its own <c>PageChanged</c> handler -- the Ready state flipping to Failed, or a route away raised from
    /// the handler -- and <c>Go</c> is suspended at an await across that whole window, so its announcement
    /// would otherwise be written AFTER <c>Dispose</c> had already cleared the region. That is exactly the
    /// orphaned standing statement rule R7 exists to prevent, left behind by the one caller that is
    /// supposed to be the region's cleaner.
    /// </summary>
    private bool disposed;

    /// <summary>
    /// Never 0. A naive ceiling division over an empty list yields 0 and the pager then reads "Page 1 of
    /// 0". An empty list renders no pager at all, so that text is unreachable today; this is what makes it
    /// unreachable rather than something the gate happens to hide.
    /// <para>
    /// The <c>Math.Max</c> is applied to the RESULT of the division and so does nothing for the division
    /// itself. <see cref="PageSize"/> is what makes this safe, by being rejected below 1 before anything
    /// reads this property.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private int PageCount => Math.Max(1, (Items.Count + PageSize - 1) / PageSize);

    /// <summary>
    /// The page actually rendered. Everything computed reads this and never <see cref="Page"/> directly,
    /// so a parent holding page 12 while a filter cuts the list to 10 titles cannot produce a grid with
    /// nothing in it under the words "Showing 661 to 720 of 10 titles. Page 12 of 1."
    /// </summary>
    private int CurrentPage => Math.Clamp(Page, 1, PageCount);

    // Rendered from strings and never from a bool: a bool attribute value is present-and-empty when true
    // and omitted when false, which ARIA reads as undefined either way. The same trap as aria-expanded.
    private string? AtFirstPage => CurrentPage <= 1 ? "true" : null;
    private string? AtLastPage => CurrentPage >= PageCount ? "true" : null;

    /// <summary>Every number on screen and every number spoken, through one format call.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Culture comes from the browser, which is correct behaviour and not a bug to stamp out: the project
    /// does not set InvariantGlobalization. All of the numbers are formatted or none of them are -- a
    /// formatted total beside an unformatted range reads "Showing 1381-1440 of 1.456 titles." under de-DE.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Number(int value) => value.ToString("N0");

    /// <summary>
    /// Singular and plural, per rule R6. The regions are atomic, so the whole sentence is re-read, and a
    /// filter narrowing the list to one title would otherwise say "of 1 titles" in both copies.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ONLY THE VISIBLE SENTENCE CAN EVER BE SINGULAR, and that is worth writing down because a test
    /// covering both forms of the spoken copy would be covering a case that cannot occur. The spoken copy
    /// comes from <see cref="Go"/>, <c>Go</c> runs only on a page change, and a page change needs at least
    /// <see cref="PageSize"/> + 1 titles to exist at all -- so the total <c>Go</c> reads is never 1.
    /// The selection still belongs here rather than at the two call sites: one property feeds both copies,
    /// which is what stops them drifting, and Task 8 filtering down to a single title makes the visible
    /// singular real.
    /// </remarks>
    private string Noun => Items.Count == 1 ? "title" : "titles";

    private int FirstShownOn(int page) => Items.Count == 0 ? 0 : (page - 1) * PageSize + 1;

    private int LastShownOn(int page) => Math.Min(page * PageSize, Items.Count);

    /// <summary>
    /// The pager's own visible page indicator. It is the ONLY place the page number appears: the count
    /// sentence deliberately does not repeat it, or it would be on screen twice and read twice. It is also
    /// the non-colour cue for which bound the user is at, which is what keeps the greyed button clear of
    /// SC 1.4.1 Use of Color -- aria-disabled cannot do that job, because it does nothing at all for a
    /// sighted user with low vision or a colour-vision deficiency.
    /// </summary>
    private string PageLabelFor(int page) => $"Page {Number(page)} of {Number(PageCount)}";

    /// <param name="between">
    /// What separates the two ends of the range: an en dash on screen, the word "to" when spoken.
    /// </param>
    private string RangeSentence(int page, string between) =>
        $"Showing {Number(FirstShownOn(page))}{between}{Number(LastShownOn(page))} of "
        + $"{Number(Items.Count)} {Noun}.";

    private string Announcement(int page) => $"{PageLabelFor(page)}. {RangeSentence(page, " to ")}";

    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// PageSize is validated FIRST and before anything divides by it. See the remarks on
    /// <see cref="PageSize"/> for why this throws rather than clamping, and for the negative case that is
    /// worse than the division by zero. It is a throw from a parameter hook, so it surfaces as a render
    /// failure at the one place that can fix it -- the caller's markup -- rather than as a sentence on
    /// screen that is not true.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The clamp correction raises <see cref="PageChanged"/> and announces NOTHING. A deep link to page 99
    /// is not a change the user made while reading the page, and the first render must say nothing: the
    /// route name and the page title already cover arrival. Only <see cref="Go"/> speaks.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        ArgumentOutOfRangeException.ThrowIfLessThan(PageSize, 1);

        if (Page == CurrentPage)
        {
            correctedFrom = null;
            return;
        }

        if (correctedFrom == Page) return;

        correctedFrom = Page;
        await PageChanged.InvokeAsync(CurrentPage);
    }

    /// <summary>Moves to <paramref name="target"/> if it exists, and says so.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// BOTH PAGER BUTTONS ARE ALWAYS RENDERED, ALWAYS ENABLED AND ALWAYS FOCUSABLE, and at a bound carry
    /// aria-disabled while this method ignores the click. The disabled ATTRIBUTE would be a trap: press
    /// Next on the second-to-last page and the button Blazor re-renders is disabled, a disabled button is
    /// out of the tab order, so the element holding focus leaves the tab order and focus falls back to the
    /// document body -- no focus indicator anywhere on the page, and the user's next Tab restarts from
    /// above the skip link. For someone paging through 25 pages by keyboard that happens twice a visit,
    /// silently. This is a house rule justified by that observable failure and NOT by a success criterion:
    /// none of them cleanly applies, because after the disable no component has focus at all. WAI-ARIA
    /// defines aria-disabled for exactly "unavailable but still perceivable and focusable", so this is the
    /// specified mechanism rather than a workaround.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// This SUPERSEDES rule R9's focus-move clause for the pager, and R9 names "Next on the last page" as
    /// its own example, so the departure has to be written down or the next reader adds a gratuitous focus
    /// jump. R9's remedy -- move focus deliberately and say what happened -- presumes the control was
    /// removed or disabled. Nothing is removed or disabled here, so there is no lost focus to repair; R9's
    /// second half, saying what happened, is honoured by the announcement below.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The announcement is the LAST statement, which is rule R4: a message raised before the new state has
    /// been asked for starts its settle timer while the clearing render is still queued, and R4's own worst
    /// case is "a slow phone re-rendering a full page of tiles" -- this page. It is built from
    /// <paramref name="target"/> rather than from <see cref="CurrentPage"/>, because the new parameter has
    /// not flowed down yet when the callback returns.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task Go(int target)
    {
        // At a bound the click still arrives, because the button is not disabled, and is ignored here.
        // Nothing is raised, nothing is said, and focus stays exactly where the user put it.
        if (target < 1 || target > PageCount) return;

        await PageChanged.InvokeAsync(target);

        // The parent may have unmounted this grid while that await was suspended -- Ready flipping to
        // Failed, or a route away raised from its handler -- in which case Dispose has already cleared the
        // region and speaking now would put a standing statement back into it with nobody left to clear it.
        // R7's whole point. The clear is the correct final state here: the page the sentence describes is
        // not on screen.
        if (disposed) return;

        Announcer.Status(Announcement(target));
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Rule R7: whoever writes a standing statement clears it. The announce and this clear are in the SAME
    /// component on purpose -- putting the clear on the page would leave the grid's sentence standing in
    /// the announcer any time the grid were unmounted while the page lived on, describing a page of tiles
    /// that is no longer rendered. An empty string is a cancellation rather than a message: it takes effect
    /// at once and speaks nothing, which is the only thing a dispose may do.
    /// <para>
    /// The flag is set before the clear and not after, because <see cref="Go"/> reads it: a grid unmounted
    /// from inside the parent's own PageChanged handler would otherwise have its announcement land after
    /// this clear and stand there uncancelled.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        disposed = true;
        Announcer.Status("");
    }
}
